What is the second energy level in the electron configuration model and what is its significance?
The second energy level in the electron configuration model corresponds to the n = 2 principal energy level. 

In this energy level, there are 8 orbitals (4 s and 3 p orbitals) that can hold a maximum of 8 electrons. The electron configuration for the second energy level is 2s² 2p⁶. 

The significance of the second energy level is that it is the second energy level that electrons occupy in an atom, after the first energy level (n = 1). The electrons in the second energy level are farther away from the nucleus than those in the first energy level, and they have a higher energy than those in the first energy level.
