When was the post-9/11 GI Bill signed into law, and what were the key provisions of the bill?
The post-9/11 GI Bill was signed into law in July 2008 by President George W. Bush. The main provisions of the act include:

1. Funding 100% of a public four-year undergraduate education to a veteran who has served three years on active duty since September 11, 2001.
2. The ability for the veteran to transfer benefits to a spouse or children after serving (or agreeing to serve) ten years.
3. Tiered education benefits based on the number of days served on active duty, providing a benefit package that gives current and previously activated National Guard and Reserve members the same benefits as active-duty members.
