What is a nuclear family, and how does it differ from other types of families?
A nuclear family is a family group consisting of two parents and their children (one or more). It is in contrast to a single-parent family, a larger extended family, and a family with more than two parents. Typically, a nuclear family centers on a married couple and may have any number of children, including biological, stepchildren, and adopted children.
