What is a single-parent family, and what are its common characteristics?
A single-parent family is a family unit consisting of one parent, often a woman, who raises one or more children without the assistance of the other biological parent. Common characteristics of a single-parent family include:

*   One parent caring for one or more children
*   The absence of the other biological parent, due to reasons such as:
    *   Divorce or break-up
    *   Death of one parent
    *   Abandonment by one parent
    *   Single-parent adoption
    *   Artificial insemination
*   The single parent often manages all responsibilities of child rearing without financial or emotional assistance
*   Historically, single-parent families have been common due to parental mortality rates (due to disease, wars, and maternal mortality)
