Describe the process by which visual information is transmitted and processed within these neural layers.
Based on the provided context, here is the process by which visual information is transmitted and processed within the neural layers:

Visual information is first perceived by the retina, where photo-receptors absorb light and transmit signals to bipolar cells, which then send signals to ganglion cells. The ganglion cells transmit this information to the brain through the optic nerve. The information then travels to the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus and then to the primary visual cortex. In the cortex, the information is processed in the occipito-temporal cortex, where a representation of the object as a whole is created. Further processing occurs in the posterior parietal cortex (dorsal stream), where the representation of an object's shape is formed using motion-based information. The ventral stream, also known as the "what" pathway, is involved in the processing of visual object identification information, and various regions within this stream have been observed to have functional specialization in functional imaging studies.
