What is the definition of a 'no-hit' game in baseball, and how is it recorded in the sport's history, specifically for the New York Giants team? 
A no-hitter in baseball is a game in which a team that batted in at least nine innings recorded no hits. A pitcher who prevents the opposing team from achieving a hit is said to have "thrown a no-hitter." This is a rare accomplishment for a pitcher or pitching staff. 

In the context of the New York Giants team, the first player to throw a no-hitter was Ed Crane, who accomplished this feat on September 27, 1888, in a seven-inning game that was called due to darkness.
