What is the historical context and purpose of the Royal Flying Doctor Service?
The Royal Flying Doctor Service (RFDS) was established by Reverend John Flynn on 15 May 1928 in Cloncurry, Queensland, Australia. Its historical context and purpose are rooted in providing medical support to isolated communities in rural and remote areas of Australia. Reverend Flynn had a vision to use radios and aircraft to connect doctors and patients in these areas, and the service was initially developed to use two-way radios and a de Havilland DH-50 aircraft to provide healthcare to people living in the Outback. The RFDS continues to operate as a non-profit organisation, providing emergency and primary health care services to those living in rural, remote, and regional areas of Australia who cannot access a hospital or general practice due to the vast distances.
