What was the significance of the Battle of Yorktown?
The Battle of Yorktown was a decisive victory by a combined force of American Continental Army troops and French Army troops over a British Army, marking the culmination of the Yorktown campaign and the last major land battle of the American Revolutionary War in North America. This victory led to the surrender of British Lt. Gen. Charles Cornwallis, the fall of the government of Lord North, and ultimately, British recognition of American independence with the 1783 Treaty of Paris.
