Who was Aurangazeb and what is his relevance to Indian history?
Aurangazeb was the last of the great Mughal emperors who ruled India from 1658 to 1707. He is considered India's most controversial king due to his less tolerant policies, reintroduction of the "jizya" tax, and destruction of several historical temples. However, he also employed significantly more Hindus in his imperial bureaucracy and opposed Sunni Muslim bigotry against Hindus and Shia Muslims. His reign is seen as a turning point in Indian history, marking the start of the decline of the Mughal empire and the beginning of the Modern Indian history era.
