What are the linguistic and etymological roots of the surname Hanes, and how do they relate to its geographical distribution?
The surname Haines (or Hanes) has linguistic and etymological roots in Old German and Old Norse. It is a patronymic form of the medieval male given name "Hain", derived from the Old German "Hagano", which originally meant "hawthorn". This name was later popularized by the Normans in England. Additionally, Haine may be derived from the Old Norse "Haghni" or "Hagne". 

The geographical distribution of the surname Haines/Hanes is most concentrated in Wales, particularly in Gwynedd, where it is the 6th most common surname. Other notable concentrations are found in Merseyside, Cheshire, West Yorkshire, Surrey, and Essex in Great Britain.
